# Change Log

## 0.1.0 (2026-08-12)

### Added
- `device://session_info` resource: current session metadata (session_id,
  ring-buffer depth, stored log rows) as JSON. Resource catalog extracted
  into module-level `build_resources`/`render_resource` for testability.
- Skill file (`embpilot-device-debugging`) synced with the new tools
  (`read_output`, `batch`, `serve`, `run`, flags, `help`) and a CLI-only
  agent section for clients without MCP support.
- Decided against monitor `--until/--timeout`: `read_output` already covers
  the wait-until-marker semantics across every entry point.
- `search_history_logs` accepts an optional `session_id`; closed sessions are
  opened read-only and searched without touching the active connection.
- Schema-driven flags for `embpilot tool` (`--port COM3 --baudrate 9600`);
  generated from the tool's JSON Schema, kebab-case, enums/types enforced,
  explicit flags override `--json` keys. Flag dests are prefixed
  (`schema_`) so `--command` cannot collide with the subcommand dest.
- `embpilot run --connect '<json>' cmd1 cmd2 ...`: connect, run commands,
  disconnect as one batch (exit code and envelope contracts identical to
  batch). `--interface` picks serial/ssh/telnet.
- SessionDatabase gained `open(readonly=True)` (no schema init, no WAL
  checkpoint on close) for safe closed-session searches.
- Prompt catalog expanded from 2 to 7 templates: `connect_and_explore`,
  `capture_boot_log`, `diagnose_connection`, `design_expect`, and
  `session_handoff`; each message carries an actionable tool sequence
  (prompts extracted into module-level `build_prompts`/`render_prompt`).
- Tool descriptions now carry structured guidance (`When to use`, `Avoid
  when`, `Typical flow`, `Pitfalls`) generated by one helper in
  `mcp_contracts.py`, improving agent tool selection.
- Connection-failure suggestions are classified (timeout / auth / refused)
  instead of the previous generic hint; timed-out `send_command` results
  append a hint when no `expect_regex` was given.
- CLI guidance: `embpilot help <tool>` shows the schema, defaults, enums,
  examples, and guidance; `embpilot tools` now prints one example per tool.
- Maintainability: split cli.py into cli.py (parser/tool dispatch, 645
  lines) + cli_daemon.py (detached daemon lifecycle, pid probing, endpoint
  validation/reachability); split server.py into session_manager.py
  (business layer: connections, pipeline, persistence) + server.py (MCP
  adapter + resource/prompt catalogs, 351 lines) with backward-compatible
  re-exports.
- `embpilot help` with no argument now lists the available tools instead of
  failing with an argparse error.
- `serve --daemon` prints the real interpreter pid from daemon.pid instead
  of the Popen stub pid (Anaconda venv redirector), so users manage the
  correct process; regression-tested.
- Critical Windows fix: `_pid_alive` never uses `os.kill` on Windows
  anymore - CPython implements os.kill with TerminateProcess for every
  signal (sig=0 included), so the old probe actually KILLED the running
  daemon during the idempotent `serve --daemon` re-run. Now uses
  OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION) + GetExitCodeProcess
  (STILL_ACTIVE) via ctypes; POSIX keeps os.kill(pid, 0) and treats
  PermissionError as alive.
- `serve --daemon` readiness now requires the endpoint file AND an actual
  connection probe (`_endpoint_reachable`), not just file presence, so the
  redirector-stub pid mismatch on Anaconda venvs cannot cause false
  success/failure.
- Idempotence regression test now verifies the first daemon survives and
  stays reachable after the second `--daemon` call; added a probe-only
  unit test proving `_pid_alive` does not terminate its target.
- `serve --daemon` (spec 5.2): detaches a serve subprocess into the
  background, writes <data-dir>/daemon.pid + serve.log, waits until the
  endpoint file exists, then exits 0. Idempotent: a second --daemon against
  a live daemon reports "already running". The child reuses the current
  interpreter (sys.executable) so it runs this exact environment; on
  Anaconda venvs the redirector-stub pid chain is handled correctly. Added
  `daemon.pid` write/cleanup in run_serve.
- High-throughput test made load-immune (poll for queue drain instead of a
  fixed sleep).
- Codex review round fixes:
  - `--socket` tool calls now merge schema flags exactly like local ones
    (flags were silently dropped over the socket, violating equivalence).
  - `search_history_logs` / `export_session` report NOT_FOUND for unknown
    session ids (were INVALID_ARGUMENT).
  - `batch --json-output` accepted (batch always prints JSONL) so the spec
    example runs unchanged.
  - CLI emits UTF-8 on Windows (reconfigured stdout/stderr): non-ASCII
    system messages (Chinese serial errors) no longer garble under agents.
  - `serve` refuses non-loopback TCP endpoints; README documents the
    local-only trust model.
  - `embpilot shell` accepts `help <tool>`; `embpilot tool <name> --help`
    shows the full tool help instead of argparse usage.
  - `run --connect` is optional (spec: omitted = no connection); run is
    fail-fast so a failed connect does not cascade NO_ACTIVE_DEVICE noise.
  - All 11 tool descriptions now carry Pitfalls; `device://session_info`
    includes interface, device, start time, status, and recent error
    patterns.
  - `run_uninstall` skips unsupported locations like `run_install` does.
  - Deduplicated: interactive install/uninstall share one flow; local and
    socket one-shot share argument parsing/exit-code mapping; batch and the
    daemon share the JSONL request parser; targets share instructions
    upsert/remove helpers.
  - Fixed trailing whitespace in change.log.
- Review round fixes: `--socket` calls against a dead daemon now report
  "cannot reach daemon" (exit 1) instead of a traceback; `reset_target`
  respects the session line ending (crlf consoles no longer get a bare
);
  `DbConsumer` keeps a strong reference to its flush tasks (asyncio GC
  pitfall); `disconnect_device` bounds the background-task wait to 2s and
  logs stragglers instead of hanging (Windows proactor wait_for/cancel race);
  dead config fields removed.
- Interactive installer cancellations: every prompt accepts `q`/`quit`/
  `cancel`/`esc`/`exit`, Ctrl+C and EOF (Ctrl+D / closed pipe) cancel the
  flow cleanly - nothing is written and the CLI reports "cancelled" (exit
  130 for Ctrl+C in non-interactive mode). EOF no longer falls through to
  defaults. ASCII punctuation used in all installer output for GBK
  consoles.
- Interactive installer mode: `embpilot install` / `uninstall` with no
  `--target` runs a CodeGraph-style picker (zero-dependency, stdlib input):
  detected harnesses are listed pre-checked, targets are selected by number,
  scope is chosen with per-target support hints, and the exact file list is
  previewed for confirmation BEFORE anything is written (cancelling writes
  nothing). `--yes` skips all prompts for scripting; non-interactive flags
  (`--target`, `--location`, `--check`, `--print-config`) are unchanged.
- Installer targets expanded to the mainstream harnesses, each with the
  MCP entry where the harness supports it plus AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md
  instructions and the skill where applicable:
  - zcode (Z.ai): ~/.zcode/cli/config.json `mcp.servers` (global) /
    ./.agents/mcp.json `mcpServers` (local, the shape ZCode scans) +
    AGENTS.md + ~/.zcode/skills/ skill copy
  - opencode: opencode.json `mcp.<name>` wrapper (argv command array +
    enabled flag) + AGENTS.md; XDG_CONFIG_HOME honored
  - codex: ~/.codex/config.toml `[mcp_servers.embpilot]` TOML table +
    AGENTS.md (global only)
  - claude / pi / agents as before
- Fixed TOML section boundaries to follow TOML semantics (table = header to
  the next header) so uninstall removes the whole table, and body lines are
  indented.
- `embpilot install` / `embpilot uninstall` (modeled on CodeGraph's
  installer): marker-fenced instructions blocks are upserted into each
  harness's CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md, listing both the MCP tools and their CLI
  equivalents so agents fall back to the CLI when MCP is unavailable.
  Targets: `claude` (MCP entry in .mcp.json / ~/.claude.json + CLAUDE.md),
  `pi` (AGENTS.md + the device-debugging skill copied into
  ~/.pi/agent/skills/), `agents` (project AGENTS.md). Flags: `--target`
  auto/all/none/comma list, `--location global|local`, `--check`,
  `--print-config <target>`. Uninstall removes only what install wrote;
  sibling MCP servers and unrelated markdown are preserved.
- The pi device-debugging skill template ships inside the package
  (`embpilot.installer.skill_template`) for skill installation.
- `embpilot serve` daemon: one persistent `SessionManager` shared across
  invocations. POSIX listens on a unix socket; Windows falls back to TCP
  loopback on 127.0.0.1 (no named-pipe server API in the standard library).
  The real endpoint is written to `<data-dir>/daemon.json` for discovery.
- `--socket` client forwarding: `tool` / `tools` / `batch` can talk to a
  running daemon (`unix:PATH`, `tcp:HOST:PORT`, or the daemon.json file path)
  with the same exit-code and output contracts as local execution.
- RPC layer (`rpc.py`): JSONL wire protocol with per-connection `id`
  correlation, serialized dispatch across concurrent clients, and `text`
  payloads for terminal rendering.
- `embpilot batch`: scripted JSONL mode (one request object per stdin line,
  one result envelope per stdout line, no banner). `--fail-fast` stops at the
  first failing tool call. Exit codes: 0 all ok / 1 tool failure / 2 malformed
  input or unknown tool.
- `read_output` tool: observe device output without sending any bytes; returns
  early when `expect_regex` matches or the `duration_ms` window elapses.
- Shared line-driven dispatch core (`cli_loop.py`) extracted from the REPL so
  `shell`, `batch`, and the future `serve` daemon behave identically.
- CLI mode: `embpilot tools`, `embpilot tool <name> --json '<args>'` (one-shot),
  and `embpilot shell` (interactive REPL). The CLI reuses the MCP dispatch
  layer, so every advertised tool behaves identically outside MCP.
- One-shot exit-code contract (0 success / 1 tool failure / 2 usage or argument
  error) and `--json-output` for the structured `ok`/`data`/`error` envelope.
- Background `monitor` mode in `embpilot shell`: new device log lines stream as
  `[log]` while tool calls stay usable (results prefixed `[cmd]`); `stop`
  exits monitoring.

### Changed
- CLI entry point restructured into subcommands; `embpilot` with no arguments
  still starts the MCP stdio server (backward compatible).
### Fixed
- mcp SDK 2.x incompatibility that crashed CLI tool calls
  (`AttributeError: 'Tool' object has no attribute 'inputSchema'`): the shared
  contract/CLI layer now resolves `input_schema`/`structured_content` across
  mcp 1.x and 2.x via `mcp_compat.py`.
- Pinned `mcp>=1.0.0,<2` (server runtime tracks mcp 1.x; CLI tolerates 2.x).
- Protocol-specific MCP tools for Serial, SSH, and Telnet with strict JSON
  Schemas, defaults, examples, and unknown-field rejection.
- Structured MCP success/error envelopes with stable error codes, retryability,
  and recovery suggestions.
- Command execution module with configurable line endings, cursor-based output
  capture, expect matching, timeout state, and output truncation state.
- Repository-level EmbPilot device-debugging agent skill and stdio MCP, CLI,
  session, and wheel integration coverage.
- Repository contributor guidance in `AGENTS.md`, including project layout,
  development commands, test conventions, and contribution workflow.
- Project skeleton: pyproject.toml, package layout, CLI entry point
- Configuration management with XDG-compliant platform paths
- Dual-track database architecture:
  - `MainDatabase` (embpilot_main.db): sessions index + operation_history
  - `SessionDatabase` (session_<ts>_<device>.db): per-connection high-frequency device_logs
- Frame assembly engine (byte buffer + configurable timeout-based flush)
- In-memory ring buffer (deque, maxlen=2000) for live log browsing
- Async producer-consumer pipeline (LogProducer, DbConsumer → SessionDatabase)
- Auto-flush DbConsumer with periodic timer (50ms interval, batch_size=200)
- Hardware drivers: SerialDevice (pyserial-asyncio), TelnetDevice (telnetlib3), SshDevice (asyncssh)
- MCP Server integration with 10 tools, 3 resources, 2 prompts:
  - Tools: connect_serial, connect_ssh, connect_telnet, disconnect_device,
    send_command, reset_target, search_history_logs, list_sessions,
    delete_session, export_session
  - Resources: device://live_log, device://sysinfo, device://analytics
  - Prompts: analyze_crash_log, hardware_sanity_check
- Session lifecycle management (auto-disconnect on reconnect, auto-cleanup on startup)
- Retention policy: configurable max_days (30) + max_gb (5) with auto-cleanup
- Local RAG engine: fastembed + LanceDB for semantic document search
- Tests covering CLI, packaging, MCP stdio/contracts, session commands, drivers,
  database, pipeline, RAG, and high-throughput simulation

### Changed
- RAG dependencies are optional through `embpilot[rag]`; default installation is
  limited to the MCP, database, and transport runtime.
- CLI help/version parsing no longer imports the full MCP server runtime.
- SSH uses AsyncSSH host-key verification defaults unless insecure bypass is
  explicitly requested.
- Telnet and SSH sessions now use byte streams consistently, and SSH requests
  the target's default shell instead of assuming `bash` exists.
- Live-log resources are documented as readable rather than falsely advertised
  as push-subscribable.
- Database schema split from single file to dual-track (main + session)
- CLI: --db-path → --main-db-path + --session-data-dir + --retention-days/--retention-max-gb
[2026-06-27]
- repo init prep: added `.gitignore` for local caches/generated files, added `.editorconfig` to pin UTF-8/LF defaults, and added `PROGRESS.md` to track initialization status plus current pytest import-path pitfall.
- runtime rearchitecture design: added a written spec covering standard PyPI package structure, runtime module boundaries, session dispatcher fan-out, `device://session_info`, and staged migration/testing strategy.
- runtime rearchitecture implementation plan: added a task-by-task execution plan with TDD steps, verification commands, and commit boundaries for the staged refactor.

### Fixed
- Built wheels now include `schema_main.sql` and `schema_session.sql`.
- The first command in a session now captures output even when the ring buffer
  was initially empty, and expect matches return before the full timeout.
- Operation history no longer stores plaintext command contents.
